<time datetime="2023-01-16">January 16, 2023</time>
<article id="WiFi" class="WebBits">
    <h2>TRY: Determine WiFi version</h2>
    <p>
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wi-Fi_6" rel="external noopener" target="_blank"
           title="Wi-Fi 6 - Wikipedia">
            Wi-Fi
            6/6E
        </a>
        is the most recent standard of wireless communication. It is capable
        of up to 9.6 Gbit/s bandwidth. Commonly,
        <abbr title="Wireless Fidelity">Wi-Fi</abbr> networks have the network
        name &lpar;known as the
        <abbr title="Service Set Identifier">SSID</abbr>&rpar; visible to make
        it so that you and others can connect your devices.
    </p>
    <aside class="exampleBlue">
        <p>
            Household routers are often a combination of an
            <abbr title="Access Point">AP</abbr> and a
            <abbr title="Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol">DHCP</abbr>
            server.
        </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
        Packets are sent using varying radio channels on 2.4/5/6 GHz ranges.
    </p>
    <aside class="examplePink">
        <p>
            Common channels combinations are <strong>1, 6, 11</strong> of
            <em>2.4GHz</em> networks.
        </p>
        <p>
            5GHz channels include <strong>36, 40, 44, and 48</strong>, whereby
            each channel is separated by <em>20MHz.<sup>1</sup> </em>
        </p>
    </aside>
    <p class="citations">
        <strong>Citations:</strong><br>
        <sup>[1]</sup>
        <cite>
            <a href="https://documentation.meraki.com/MR/Wi-Fi_Basics_and_Best_Practices/Channel_Planning_Best_Practices"
               rel="external noopener" target="_blank" title="Channel Planning Best Practices - Cisco Meraki">
                <q>Channel Planning Best Practices - Cisco Meraki</q>
            </a>
        </cite>
    </p>
</article>
